When you use my link, I earn a small commission —

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:46.739
so it's also a great way to support the channel.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:47.941
Alright, now back to the video.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.678
To understand what to invest in,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:55.281
you first need to understand what this race

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:55.748
is actually about.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:56.649
Long-time viewers know

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.685
I've been talking about ideas like this for a while.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.221
Last year I made a video called All in AI —

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.357
why you should start Vibe Coding right now.

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:05.959
I put forward one core thesis:

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:09.162
the golden age of the "tool person" is being ended by AI.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.163
What does that mean?

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.899
At its core, there are only two ways

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:13.299
to build wealth:

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.702
one is labor — your physical effort, brainpower, time —

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:19.539
the other is capital — assets that earn money for you.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.307
But the vast majority of people rely on the former.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.277
We're essentially "tool people," tied to some skill or tool,

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.346
trading it for income.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.547
And that path used to work,

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:30.049
because no matter how powerful machines got, they always needed people to operate,

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.418
maintain, and connect them. The finer the division of labor,

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.420
the more demand there was for tool people.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.723
But AI is changing the rules.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:40.159
AI is fundamentally different from past tech revolutions. The steam engine,

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.829
electricity, the internet — all partial automation.

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.297
Machines handled part of the work, but couldn't function without humans.

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:47.066
AI is the starting point toward full automation.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:47.767
In the digital realm,

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.370
it can already handle knowledge work end-to-end.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.471
And in the physical world,

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:54.007
the rise of humanoid robots means physical labor may now be

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.275
on the chopping block too.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:55.842
Half a year later,

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.443
these predictions are being validated one by one.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.212
The most direct evidence: this past February,

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.614
after Anthropic dropped Claude's new capabilities,

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.318
global software and services stocks lost nearly

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:07.587
a trillion dollars in market cap in a single week.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:09.389
What does that tell us?

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:11.858
The market finally got it:

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.860
the model itself is the app.

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:17.330
Every model upgrade directly eats into the functionality

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:17.864
of the application layer.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:20.466
Those SaaS companies that seemed to have moats —

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:24.103
they're just the tool layer. And the tool layer is collapsing.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.206
And the tool people dependent on that tool layer?

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.275
Their situation can only get harder.

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.877
Layoffs at Silicon Valley giants have never stopped,

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:33.646
and every single business owner is thinking about how to use AI

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:34.047
to cut costs and boost efficiency.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.916
That's the underlying logic behind Peter Levels' tweet —

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.986
once AGI arrives, capital won't need labor anymore.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:40.620
Your physical strength,

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:43.756
your brainpower — none of it will ticket you into the wealth game.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:46.092
At that point, the only thing keeping you at the table

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.527
is the assets you own.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:50.229
Where you stand right now is your starting position

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:50.797
entering that new world.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:54.901
That's why everyone is running this race.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.736
But hold on — didn't Elon Musk say this?

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:59.305
[laughs] After AGI, abundance for everyone —

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:01.574
no need to work, everyone gets

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.209
Universal Basic Income,

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.745
just go do what you love.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:07.413
Honestly, I want to believe that too.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.848
Who wouldn't want to live in that world?

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.483
But ask yourself honestly:

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.353
do you actually think that's going to happen?

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:14.821
About the future of AGI,

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:18.257
the only thing we know for sure is that nobody knows for sure.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:21.194
And that uncertainty is exactly what's driving so much anxiety.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:22.328
So what do we do?

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.864
I want to borrow something someone figured out

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:25.965
over 300 years ago —

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:27.767
Pascal's Wager.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:30.336
Pascal faced the ultimate question:

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:33.740
does God exist? And his answer was:

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.641
you don't need to know the answer.

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.876
You just need to figure out which choice

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:37.577
costs you the least.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:39.612
Believe — if God exists, you go to heaven.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.448
If he doesn't, you lose nothing. Don't believe — if he exists,

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:43.216
you're in big trouble.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:46.619
So regardless of the truth, believing is always the better bet.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.388
Now apply that to AI. You have two choices:

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:51.557
position yourself now, or do nothing and wait to see.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.560
If the future locks in a hierarchy and you've built up assets,

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:56.129
you win.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.463
If the future is universal abundance,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.999
what you built beforehand didn't hurt you —

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:01.200
no loss at all.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.669
But if you do nothing,

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.405
and that utopia turns out to be wishful thinking?

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.807
You're locked into a position you don't want to be in,

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:08.708
with no way out.

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:11.844
So whether you're an optimist or a pessimist,

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:14.681
the rational choice is the same: position yourself first.

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.883
But here's the question: how, specifically?

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.184
How do you stake that claim?

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:20.987
Since we're in this race,

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:23.790
the most obvious move is to just buy AI stocks, right?

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.092
But there's a question most people haven't really thought through:

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.127
which layer exactly are you buying?

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:31.030
A lot of companies you think are AI-related

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:34.233
are actually the very ones being disrupted by AI.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.368
There's an important concept here:

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:37.570
AI-native.

00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:40.373
Right now, there are two types of companies doing "AI."

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.543
Type one: bolting AI onto an old architecture.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:44.277
Think

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:47.113
Salesforce, Notion, QuickBooks, Adobe.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.116
They have massive user bases and years of accumulated product

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:50.650
architecture.

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:52.351
In the past, those were core assets.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:54.487
But in the AI era, those have become liabilities,

00:05:54.554 --> 00:05:57.757
because these structures represent an existing way of producing

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.058
and organizing resources —

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.427
one that's already outdated. But these companies

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:04.430
can't let go, and can't afford to. So they patch and tinker,

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.399
force-fitting AI on top.

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:07.600
That's not AI-native.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:10.136
That's putting a motor on a horse-drawn carriage.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.404
Type two:

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.506
built for AI from day one.

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:17.076
The entire system architecture is designed around AI

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:17.176
from the ground up.

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:20.179
AI is the engine. Everything else is the drivetrain.

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:22.648
The starting point isn't "how do we add AI to our product?" —

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.317
it's "AI is the product.

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:26.018
Everything else revolves around it."

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:27.820
The difference between these two is fundamental.

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.422
Take an example: you want to control a smart speaker at home.

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:31.424
Why do you have to open that clunky

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:34.594
app? An AI assistant handles it directly — you don't even

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:35.394
need that app.

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:36.496
Or take taxes.

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.798
Tax season is coming up. All you want is

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.166
an accurate tax return.

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:42.101
Why do you care what the software interface looks like?

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:44.737
AI does the math, fills it in, submits it. Done.

00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:47.907
That's the AI-native mindset: outcome-driven.

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:49.876
You want the result, not the process.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.578
All those menus, buttons, forms, workflows, interfaces —

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:55.348
they existed to help humans operate a system.

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.116
But once AI replaces the operator,

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:59.252
those things lose their reason to exist.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.420
Now I'm not saying

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.889
software or interfaces will disappear entirely.

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:06.392
Consumer-facing software and interfaces won't go away —

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:08.628
people still want great visual experiences.

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:11.264
But the interfaces and software designed for human operators

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.734
and producers — those are disappearing.

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:18.538
And what this reflects is a fundamental shift in how value is created.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.473
When you view the market through this lens,

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.476
you'll find that the vast majority of so-called "AI stocks"

00:07:23.543 --> 00:07:24.710
aren't AI-native at all.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.647
They've just wrapped AI around old products.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.817
Truly AI-native companies are just starting to emerge,

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:32.318
and there are far fewer than you'd think.

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:34.787
Now, this doesn't mean every legacy app company will die.

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:35.721
Some will survive,

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:38.357
but they'll go through an incredibly painful transformation.

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:39.192
And even if they make it,

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.893
their scale probably won't be what you'd expect,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.329
because AI has reset them back to the starting line,

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:46.065
competing neck-and-neck with AI-native startups.

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:47.900
So when you say "I want to invest in AI,"

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:48.901
the first question to ask yourself is:

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.803
am I investing in AI itself,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.873
or in the old world being disrupted by AI?

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.842
But that's only half the story.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:57.677
What we've discussed is mostly the software side.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:07:59.478
There's something you can't quite see yet,

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:01.080
but whose impact will be even greater:

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:02.715
physical-world AI.

00:08:04.517 --> 00:08:06.285
There's a question a lot of people have

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:07.420
that no one's really answered clearly:

00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:09.188
AI is so powerful now —

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:10.289
has productivity actually improved?

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:11.791
Is it showing up in GDP?

00:08:11.858 --> 00:08:14.894
Andrej Karpathy, in his Dwarkesh Podcast

00:08:14.961 --> 00:08:16.462
interview, asked the same question.

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:24.837
He builds AI himself,

00:08:24.904 --> 00:08:29.075
and even he's asking: can AI push GDP growth to

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:30.243
10%?

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:31.377
My take:

00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:33.880
the real productivity unlock won't come from digital AI —

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.715
it'll come from physical-world AI.

00:08:35.781 --> 00:08:39.185
Right now, all software is fundamentally virtual.

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:39.852
It has value,

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.688
but ultimately it has to translate into action

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:43.222
in the physical world.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:45.858
Software improves decision-making, optimizes processes —

00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:48.594
but GDP ultimately comes from physical-world production:

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:50.696
building houses, roads, manufacturing goods,

00:08:50.763 --> 00:08:51.564
moving freight.

00:08:51.964 --> 00:08:55.902
When AI can truly move atoms —

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:58.771
that's when it truly unlocks productivity.

00:08:58.838 --> 00:09:00.239
So what is physical-world AI?

00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:01.874
Physical entities embedded with an AI brain

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:04.744
that can execute tasks in the real world.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:05.611
Self-driving vehicles,

00:09:05.678 --> 00:09:07.213
creating value 24/7.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.082
Humanoid robots that can use any tool or machine

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:10.750
designed for humans.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:12.051
The factories that build them.

00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:15.554
And the compute, energy, and infrastructure powering it all.

00:09:15.621 --> 00:09:18.557
When masses of AI robots are working in factories and construction sites,

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:19.825
delivering services in the physical world,

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:22.762
even running computers to operate digital-world

00:09:22.828 --> 00:09:25.698
AI — that's when the GDP growth curve jumps to another

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:26.198
dimension entirely.

00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:27.133
So as an investor,

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:29.602
keep your eye firmly on embodied AI.

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:33.139
AI moving from bits to atoms.

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:35.007
Remember what I said at the beginning?

00:09:35.074 --> 00:09:37.843
The endgame of this race is assets that grow on their own,

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.578
no longer dependent on human labor.

00:09:39.645 --> 00:09:41.347
The day embodied AI becomes widespread,

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:43.983
that endgame arrives. Factories run themselves,

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.819
fleets drive themselves, robots do the work.

00:09:46.886 --> 00:09:49.021
Whoever owns those assets is positioned.

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:50.623
And physical AI has one more key feature:

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.992
you can't easily replicate them.

00:09:53.059 --> 00:09:55.227
Copying code is free. But copying a factory,

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:56.796
a vehicle, a robot —

00:09:56.862 --> 00:09:58.798
that's not so easy in the physical world.

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:01.534
And that brings us to the third dimension:

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.103
doing things that are hard to scale.

00:10:05.571 --> 00:10:08.975
This is less about investing strategy

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:12.378
and more about what it means to be human in the age of AI.

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:15.915
AI is replacing the tool layer, replacing the tool people.

00:10:15.982 --> 00:10:17.750
So where does human value come from?

00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:19.518
When AI releases one new feature,

00:10:19.585 --> 00:10:21.454
an entire group of professionals can lose their jobs.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.489
Years of expertise you've built up —

00:10:23.556 --> 00:10:26.625
looked at through a results lens, it can suddenly lose

00:10:26.692 --> 00:10:27.460
much of its meaning.

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:28.527
Let me share my own example.

00:10:28.594 --> 00:10:31.630
[laughs] People used to call me a "knowledge creator."

00:10:31.697 --> 00:10:32.365
But starting last year,

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:35.868
I felt like purely spreading knowledge didn't have much reason

00:10:35.935 --> 00:10:36.702
to continue.

00:10:36.769 --> 00:10:38.938
Because AI already does it better than I do.

00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:40.639
I've been using NotebookLM a lot lately.

00:10:40.706 --> 00:10:43.342
It can generate tutorials, podcasts,

00:10:43.409 --> 00:10:44.377
even videos, from hundreds of sources.

00:10:44.443 --> 00:10:47.013
Knowledge has entered the era of personalization.

00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:49.649
ByteDance recently released SeedDance 2.0.

00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:52.284
The editing skills I'd always been proud of,

00:10:52.351 --> 00:10:53.919
my understanding of visual storytelling —

00:10:54.286 --> 00:10:56.555
I always thought those weren't so easy to replace.

00:10:56.622 --> 00:10:59.191
But when AI is specifically trained on those things,

00:10:59.291 --> 00:11:02.094
you realize it does it way better than me.

00:11:02.161 --> 00:11:04.563
I've also been Vibe Coding my own software.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:07.133
And now, AI building software has reached a

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:07.700
"say it and it's done" level.

00:11:07.767 --> 00:11:10.036
The other day someone used AI to clone a very well-known

00:11:10.102 --> 00:11:10.403
piece of software,

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:12.038
added new features, open-sourced it all —

00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:13.839
free for everyone to use.

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:15.241
That gave me a clear realization:

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:18.611
in the future, most software might become like writing articles

00:11:18.677 --> 00:11:18.878
or

00:11:18.944 --> 00:11:21.614
making videos — it's free. It's not a product;

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:23.115
it's a traffic channel.

00:11:23.182 --> 00:11:24.784
To borrow a line from The Three-Body Problem:

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.219
"Photography is gone. Editing is gone.

00:11:27.286 --> 00:11:28.020
Programming is gone.

00:11:28.087 --> 00:11:30.723
The thing you were good at — it's gone."

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.626
When your primary value was in providing tools,

00:11:33.692 --> 00:11:36.796
operating tools, or being the tool itself,

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:38.264
once those aren't needed anymore,

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.399
where does your value come from?

00:11:40.466 --> 00:11:44.303
I believe your real moat is the things you have

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.272
that can't easily be scaled.

00:11:46.338 --> 00:11:49.108
And here's the key concept: arbitrage.

00:11:49.175 --> 00:11:51.077
You're doing something hard to scale,

00:11:51.143 --> 00:11:53.879
but inside it you're using cutting-edge AI to automate,

00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:57.283
cut costs, and boost output. That gap is where huge arbitrage lives.

00:11:57.349 --> 00:11:58.617
Say you have a physical business.

00:11:58.684 --> 00:12:01.353
AI hasn't eaten into it yet. Hard to immediately replace.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.355
It might not look "sexy" —

00:12:03.422 --> 00:12:06.792
hard to scale. But that's exactly your moat.

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:08.627
Use AI to automate the back end, cut out the tool layer.

00:12:08.694 --> 00:12:10.463
While your competitors haven't realized this yet,

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:12.531
you've already got the edge.

00:12:12.598 --> 00:12:15.201
So what kinds of things are hard to scale?

00:12:16.001 --> 00:12:17.837
First: genuine human relationships.

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:20.840
AI can help you "handle" ten thousand people at once,

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:23.142
but that's not a relationship — that's customer service.

00:12:23.209 --> 00:12:27.379
Real trust and community require your actual presence.

00:12:27.446 --> 00:12:30.316
Second: businesses that deal with the physical world.

00:12:30.382 --> 00:12:32.384
Running a hardware company, a brick-and-mortar business,

00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:34.587
a physical space — naturally constrained by physics.

00:12:34.653 --> 00:12:37.256
But you can use AI inside them to automate operations,

00:12:37.323 --> 00:12:39.358
cut costs, boost efficiency. That's the arbitrage.

00:12:39.425 --> 00:12:41.627
Third: your experience, judgment, and personal brand.

00:12:41.694 --> 00:12:43.996
AI can generate infinite content,

00:12:44.063 --> 00:12:45.664
but it doesn't have a real life.

00:12:45.731 --> 00:12:48.601
The mistakes you've made, the intuition extracted from failure,

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:49.468
your personality —

00:12:49.535 --> 00:12:51.937
those form the irreplaceable parts of you.

00:12:52.004 --> 00:12:52.705
So in the AI era,

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.141
lean into things that are hard to scale,

00:12:55.207 --> 00:12:56.876
and use AI inside them to arbitrage.

00:12:56.942 --> 00:13:00.613
Because the easily scalable territory

00:13:00.679 --> 00:13:01.380
already belongs to AI.

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:03.415
And that leads to a very practical question:

00:13:03.482 --> 00:13:06.719
how do you actually pull off that arbitrage?

00:13:06.785 --> 00:13:08.554
Which brings me to the fourth dimension:

00:13:08.621 --> 00:13:09.488
a thousand hours.

00:13:10.856 --> 00:13:11.791
Regular viewers know

00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:15.261
I've talked about the 10,000-hour rule from Malcolm Gladwell's Outliers —

00:13:15.327 --> 00:13:17.296
that number is definitely exaggerated,

00:13:17.363 --> 00:13:18.564
but the core idea is right:

00:13:18.631 --> 00:13:20.499
if you want to truly master something,

00:13:20.566 --> 00:13:22.768
you need to put in enough time.

00:13:22.835 --> 00:13:25.604
In the age of AI, you don't need ten thousand hours.

00:13:25.671 --> 00:13:28.941
But you need at least a thousand hours of genuinely

00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:30.276
using and studying AI.

00:13:30.342 --> 00:13:32.278
To pull off that arbitrage,

00:13:32.344 --> 00:13:35.514
you have to truly understand AI. Not just reading articles,

00:13:35.581 --> 00:13:36.782
scrolling through some tweets.

00:13:36.849 --> 00:13:39.385
Actually spending thousands of hours working alongside it,

00:13:39.451 --> 00:13:42.454
knowing what it can do, what it can't, where the edges are.

00:13:42.521 --> 00:13:45.191
And those thousand hours can also answer two questions

00:13:45.257 --> 00:13:45.958
a lot of people have:

00:13:46.025 --> 00:13:47.493
"I don't know what to invest in"

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:50.763
and "I don't have capital — how do I even invest?"

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:54.166
Let's talk about no capital first. The endgame is

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:54.967
assets doing the talking.

00:13:55.034 --> 00:13:56.468
But that endgame isn't today.

00:13:56.535 --> 00:13:59.238
Today we're still in a window. Your intelligence,

00:13:59.305 --> 00:14:01.740
labor, and creativity can still help you build capital.

00:14:01.807 --> 00:14:04.543
But the key is to use AI to amplify your output.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:05.978
The reason this window is short

00:14:06.045 --> 00:14:09.181
is that the space for earning income through pure labor

00:14:09.248 --> 00:14:09.648
keeps shrinking.

00:14:09.715 --> 00:14:10.983
But before that window closes,

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.452
AI is precisely the strongest lever you have.

00:14:13.519 --> 00:14:16.222
So the first step isn't thinking about which stock to buy —

00:14:16.288 --> 00:14:21.627
it's to use AI to build your first pot of capital outside the market.

00:14:21.927 --> 00:14:23.195
Now, about not knowing what to invest in:

00:14:23.262 --> 00:14:25.664
legendary investor Peter Lynch, in his book

00:14:25.731 --> 00:14:26.765
One Up on Wall Street,

00:14:26.832 --> 00:14:29.201
said your best investment ideas come from your experience

00:14:29.268 --> 00:14:30.402
as a user.

00:14:30.469 --> 00:14:32.037
When you genuinely use a product,

00:14:32.104 --> 00:14:34.073
you feel things others can't.

00:14:34.139 --> 00:14:37.643
And AI is something you have to be immersed in to truly understand.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.746
Those of us who live and breathe AI, scrolling Twitter every day,

00:14:40.813 --> 00:14:43.515
often feel like "a day in AI is a year in the real world" —

00:14:43.582 --> 00:14:46.418
like the whole world already knows how powerful AI is.

00:14:46.485 --> 00:14:47.720
But when you step back into real life,

00:14:47.786 --> 00:14:51.056
you realize most people around you are barely paying attention.

00:14:51.123 --> 00:14:53.459
We actually live in a very small bubble.

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:55.894
That's why some say AGI is already here —

00:14:55.961 --> 00:14:57.796
it's just not evenly distributed.

00:14:57.863 --> 00:14:58.864
But that unevenness —

00:14:58.931 --> 00:15:01.467
that information gap — is your opportunity. And this opportunity

00:15:01.533 --> 00:15:04.136
isn't just about arbitrage and investing.

00:15:04.203 --> 00:15:07.273
Those thousand hours also give you something

00:15:07.339 --> 00:15:08.007
even more important:

00:15:08.073 --> 00:15:09.108
conviction.

00:15:09.174 --> 00:15:10.976
Conviction is crucial in investing.

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:14.079
It determines whether you can hold on when others are panicking,

00:15:14.146 --> 00:15:16.282
whether you can stay the course when tempted by wrong moves.

00:15:16.348 --> 00:15:18.617
Like with my Tesla investment — I got FSD early,

00:15:18.684 --> 00:15:19.752
use it every single day.

00:15:19.818 --> 00:15:22.421
I've watched this car transform day by day.

00:15:22.488 --> 00:15:24.590
That kind of conviction can't be given to you by someone else —

00:15:24.657 --> 00:15:26.725
it comes from your own lived experience.

00:15:26.792 --> 00:15:29.461
This past half year going all-in on AI,

00:15:29.528 --> 00:15:32.164
my biggest takeaway is: you have to actually use it

00:15:32.231 --> 00:15:34.233
for your mindset to truly change.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.335
You go from someone being pushed forward

00:15:36.402 --> 00:15:38.671
to becoming an AI native.

00:15:38.737 --> 00:15:41.874
But a lot of people past 30 are reluctant to learn new things,

00:15:41.941 --> 00:15:43.275
don't want to start from zero.

00:15:43.342 --> 00:15:45.945
But when it comes to AI, the unwillingness to start over —

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:47.846
that is the real risk.

00:15:47.913 --> 00:15:51.817
So spending a thousand hours on AI is something everyone can do.

00:15:51.884 --> 00:15:54.787
The barrier to entry is extremely low; the payoff is extremely high.

00:15:54.853 --> 00:15:57.890
The only barrier is mental: resistance,

00:15:57.956 --> 00:15:59.892
laziness, and dismissiveness.

00:15:59.958 --> 00:16:01.660
Finally, on investing in your AI practice,

00:16:01.727 --> 00:16:02.895
two more points.

00:16:02.961 --> 00:16:05.698
First: don't be cheap when it comes to spending on AI.

00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:07.866
A lot of people think using free ChatGPT means

00:16:07.933 --> 00:16:08.334
they're "using AI" —

00:16:08.434 --> 00:16:10.602
they're not. Subscribe to the best models,

00:16:10.669 --> 00:16:12.204
use the most cutting-edge productivity tools.

00:16:12.271 --> 00:16:13.339
Spend a few extra dollars a month,

00:16:13.405 --> 00:16:16.508
and you'll actually know where AI's true capabilities and limits are.

00:16:16.575 --> 00:16:19.345
That's not spending — that's the most direct investment in yourself.

00:16:19.411 --> 00:16:19.545
Second:

00:16:19.611 --> 00:16:22.081
seriously integrate AI into your actual work.

00:16:22.147 --> 00:16:23.982
Don't just use it as a search engine,

00:16:24.049 --> 00:16:26.085
and don't just chase the shiny new thing,

00:16:26.151 --> 00:16:27.886
playing with flashy demos every day.

00:16:27.953 --> 00:16:30.055
What you should really do is experiment seriously —

00:16:30.122 --> 00:16:32.491
figure out how it can genuinely replace your work,

00:16:32.558 --> 00:16:33.625
cut your costs, boost your output.

00:16:33.692 --> 00:16:36.395
That's the real meaning of a thousand hours.

00:16:36.462 --> 00:16:39.264
I can promise you: once you actually try it this way,

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:41.900
your entire perspective on AI will fundamentally shift.

00:16:41.967 --> 00:16:44.503
So that's the fourth dimension I think every investor

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.637
needs to consider in the age of AI.

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:47.773
Before you think about which stock to buy,

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.375
invest in your thousand hours first.

00:16:49.441 --> 00:16:51.577
This is probably the most important investment you can make.

00:16:51.643 --> 00:16:54.246
It builds your capital outside the market, builds your information edge,

00:16:54.313 --> 00:16:55.247
and builds your conviction.

00:16:55.447 --> 00:16:58.384
Those thousand hours are your entry ticket

00:16:58.450 --> 00:16:59.585
into this race.

00:16:59.651 --> 00:17:02.020
Alright, that's everything for today's video.

00:17:02.087 --> 00:17:04.323
Hope it gave you something to think about. Thanks for watching,

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:05.157
and I'll see you next time.

00:17:05.224 --> 00:17:06.025
Take care!

